vscode: two tabs, six selections, one accidental bracket
open: order.provider.ts checkout.json errors.json

@brief
A ticket.

    ORD-552  Checkout is going live in Ireland on Monday. Orders carry
             their own currency now. Charge in the order's currency and
             stop refusing refunds on anything that is not sterling.

Three files are open. `:wn` writes the current one and moves to the next;
`:wqa` writes everything and quits. You will not need to come back to a
file once you have left it, so work in order.

  1. order.provider.ts

     - `charge` takes the currency as its second argument. It should be
       the order's own currency, not the hard-coded `'GBP'`.
     - the currency guard in `refund` goes: the block, the `if` and the
       blank line after it. Every currency is refundable now.
     - with the guard gone nothing in this file throws, so the
       `exception` import is dead. Remove the line.

  2. checkout.json

     - the template becomes `checkout-multi`
     - the supported currency list becomes `["GBP", "EUR"]`

  3. errors.json

     Leave it alone. `REFUND_CURRENCY_UNSUPPORTED` is still raised by the
     reconciliation job, so the code stays in the registry. Change this
     file and the check fails.

@keys
| `ci(` | change an argument list |
| `ci"` | change a string |
| `ci[` | change what is inside the square brackets |
| `dap` | delete a paragraph — a fenced block with a blank line after it |
| `dd` | delete a line |
| `:wn` | write this file and move to the next |
| `:wqa` | write everything and quit |

@hints
Everything in this ticket is an object you have already met. The only new
decision is which one each edit wants, and the shape of the text tells
you.
--
The guard is three lines with a blank line after it and nothing blank
inside it. That is a paragraph.
--
Do the dead import first while you can still count lines from the top of
the file, then the charge call, then the guard. In the JSON, land on a letter
inside the value itself — the colon between the key and the value is
not inside either string.

@solution
    j dd
    15j f( ci( order.id, order.currency <Esc>
    8j dap
    :wn<Enter>
    2j fc ci" checkout-multi <Esc>
    j 0 f[ ci[ "GBP", "EUR" <Esc>
    :wqa<Enter>

Ninety-two keystrokes for five edits in two files, and every one of the
five was an object plus an operator.

The part worth reviewing is the guard. It is an `if` with a body, a
condition and a blank line under it, and none of that mattered: it was
also a block of non-blank lines surrounded by blank ones, which is a
paragraph, and `dap` took it and its trailing blank line in three keys.
Reading code as paragraphs is a habit worth building — in a conventionally
formatted TypeScript file, one paragraph is usually one thought, and
whole-thought edits are exactly the ones that leave the blank lines right.

The other habit is the one the decoy is testing. Three files were open
and only two were yours. The temptation with `:wqa` is to treat every open
buffer as fair game; a real branch is reviewed by someone who will ask why
an unrelated file is in the diff.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async checkout(basket: Basket) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.create({
      values: { customerId: basket.customerId, total: basket.total },
    });

    await this.paymentService.charge(order.id, 'GBP');

    return order;
  }

  public async refund(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.currency !== 'GBP') {
      throw exception('REFUND_CURRENCY_UNSUPPORTED');
    }

    return this.paymentService.refund(order.id);
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async checkout(basket: Basket) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.create({
      values: { customerId: basket.customerId, total: basket.total },
    });

    await this.paymentService.charge(order.id, order.currency);

    return order;
  }

  public async refund(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    return this.paymentService.refund(order.id);
  }
}

@start checkout.json
{
  "checkout": {
    "template": "checkout-gbp",
    "supportedCurrencies": ["GBP"],
    "maxBasketValue": 250000
  }
}

@expected checkout.json
{
  "checkout": {
    "template": "checkout-multi",
    "supportedCurrencies": ["GBP", "EUR"],
    "maxBasketValue": 250000
  }
}

@start errors.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "REFUND_CURRENCY_UNSUPPORTED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false },
  "REFUND_FAILED": { "statusCode": 502, "sentry": true }
}
